---
import DocsLayout from "@layouts/DocsLayout.astro"
import { buildDocsJsonLd } from "../../../lib/docs-metadata"

const origin = Astro.site?.origin || "http://localhost:4321"
const title = "LyraShield for Pi — MCP and Skills Guide"
const description = "Connect Pi's built-in MCP client to LyraShield with hosted OAuth, and use the standalone CLI or CI when you prefer."
const updatedDate = "2026-10-02"
const { canonical, techArticle, breadcrumbList } = buildDocsJsonLd({
  origin,
  path: "/docs/integrations/pi",
  title,
  description,
  updatedDate,
  breadcrumbLabel: "Pi",
})
const jsonLd = [techArticle, breadcrumbList]
---

<DocsLayout title={`${title} | LyraShield AI`} description={description} canonical={canonical} jsonLd={jsonLd} updatedDate={updatedDate} currentPath="/docs/integrations/pi">
  <h1>Pi</h1>
  <p>
    Pi includes built-in MCP support over stdio and Streamable HTTP, including OAuth and can load
    Agent Skills. This guide uses the hosted OAuth endpoint. It documents setup, not a recorded
    LyraShield-authenticated Pi runtime acceptance.
  </p>
  <h2>Connect Pi to hosted MCP</h2>
  <p>
    Add the personal connection at user scope, then sign in. Pi stores OAuth credentials in its own
    user credential store; do not add a static token to the configuration.
  </p>
  <pre><code>pi mcp add lyrashield --url https://app.lyrashieldai.com/api/mcp
pi mcp list
pi mcp login lyrashield</code></pre>
  <p>
    In an interactive Pi session, <code>/mcp</code> shows connection state and tools. After the
    browser OAuth flow completes, make a read-only call such as workspace discovery before asking
    for any scan. A successful setup command alone does not prove an authenticated tool call.
  </p>
  <h2>Choose the right scope</h2>
  <p>
    Pi stores user-level servers in <code>~/.pi/agent/mcp.json</code>. For a project-shared
    endpoint, use <code>pi mcp add --local</code>; Pi reads <code>.pi/mcp.json</code> only after
    project trust is granted. Keep personal credentials at user scope. A project server entry
    replaces a user entry with the same name. A third-party extension that replaces Pi's built-in
    MCP support can change which configuration is loaded.
  </p>
  <h2>Pi skills discovery</h2>
  <p>
    Pi discovers Agent Skills from <code>~/.agents/skills/</code> and{" "}
    <code>.agents/skills/</code>. Use reviewed, versioned skill directories and inspect their
    instructions and scripts before granting project trust. Install LyraShield's shared skills
    into Pi's native paths with the pinned CLI:
  </p>
  <pre><code>npx -y lyrashield@0.2.14 skills install pi --dry-run
npx -y lyrashield@0.2.14 skills install pi</code></pre>
  <p>
    The CLI records files it owns and preserves unowned or customized files. Reload Pi and confirm
    the skills appear. LyraShield's Pi package/catalog route remains in preparation; this guide
    does not imply a public Pi listing or authenticated Pi runtime acceptance.
  </p>
  <h2>Use local CLI and CI when preferred</h2>
  <pre><code>npx -y lyrashield@0.2.14 login --oauth
npx -y lyrashield@0.2.14 check-diff
npx -y lyrashield@0.2.14 gate --verdict --commit "$(git rev-parse HEAD)"</code></pre>
  <p>
    This standalone path remains available whether or not Pi MCP is configured. Oh My Pi is a
    separate client with its own configuration paths; do not reuse Pi paths for it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="https://pi.dev/docs/latest/mcp">Pi MCP documentation</a> ·
    <a href="https://pi.dev/docs/latest/skills">Pi skills documentation</a> ·
    <a href="https://pi.dev/docs/latest/packages">Pi packages documentation</a>
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
